      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to analyze the numerator and the denominator separately and determine the intervals where the expression is non-negative.\n\n1. **Numerator**: The numerator is \\(x + 6\\).\n   - The numerator is zero when \\(x = -6\\).\n   - The numerator is positive when \\(x > -6\\) and negative when \\(x < -6\\).\n\n2. **Denominator**: The denominator is \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\).\n   - The quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) can be analyzed by completing the square or using the discriminant.\n   - The discriminant of \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is \\(2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\\). Since the discriminant is negative, the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has no real roots and is always positive (since the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is positive).\n   - Therefore, the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real \\(x\\).\n\nSince the denominator is always positive, the sign of the expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is determined by the sign of the numerator \\(x + 6\\). The numerator \\(x + 6\\) is non-negative when \\(x \\ge -6\\) and is non-positive when \\(x < -6\\).\n\nTherefore, the expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is non-negative when \\(x \\ge -6\\). In interval notation, the solution to the inequality is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
